Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "BLOCK_NUMBER": block height of the transfer – put in final SELECT and to order / group  
• "ADDRESS": contract address that emitted the event – filter to USDT contract (`'0xa614f803b6fd780986a42c78ec9c7f77e6ded13c'`)  
• "EVENT_SIGNATURE": lets us keep only ‟Transfer(address,address,uint256)” events  
• "ARGS": JSON array that contains [from, to, value] – needs `PARSE_JSON` then array subscripts `[0]`, `[1]`, `[2]`  
• "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP": optional, could be shown or used for extra filters  
• "TOPICS": alternate place to see indexed addresses, not needed once we rely on ARGS  
• "TRANSACTION_HASH" / "LOG_INDEX": can be shown for provenance if desired  

Typical conditions  
• "ADDRESS" = '0xa614f803b6fd780986a42c78ec9c7f77e6ded13c'  
• "EVENT_SIGNATURE" = 'Transfer(address,address,uint256)'        -- or its hash  
• ORDER BY (PARSE_JSON("ARGS")[2]::NUMBER / 1e6) DESC              -- biggest transfers first  
• LIMIT 3 (or LIMIT 20 for exploration queries)                    -- keep result set small  

Below are ten exploratory → final Snowflake SQL snippets (all limited to 20 rows) demonstrating how one might work toward the requested result.  
Every identifier is enclosed in double‑quotes and fully‑qualified.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at 20 random decoded events just to understand the column layout.
